"Lenses and Optical Instruments | VirtualDemos",
"Lenses and Optical Instruments",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} and §{#} to §{#}. Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Where it is in the demo:</strong> the Optics Bench, with the Converging lens, Magnifier, Microscope, Telescope, A shape, point by point and A shape in a magnifier setups.",
"<strong>Using the demo.</strong> An object arrow (or, for the telescope, a parallel beam) sends rays through one or two lenses. Click a lens to open its editor: converging or diverging, focal length and aperture. Drag an element to move it; with it selected, the arrow keys move it {#} cm, or {#} cm with Shift. With one lens, the Image section gives the object distance, the image distance and magnification found from the traced rays, and the image’s kind. With two converging lenses on one axis, the Microscope or Telescope section follows the rays through both and gives the angular magnification measured from the rays, with the eye relaxed (final image at infinity) and the near point at {#} cm.",
"<strong>What the demo models.</strong> Each lens is an ideal thin lens: it bends every ray by the thin lens rule exactly, so it has no aberrations, and its traced images agree with the thin lens equation. The microscope’s magnification is the angle of the rays leaving the eyepiece divided by the angle the object would fill at the near point; the telescope’s is the angle of the beam leaving the eyepiece divided by the angle it comes in at (as tangents). A shape source (a flat house, letter F or square lying in the plane of the drawing) is imaged point by point: five rays from each point of its outline, and the image point where they meet; the Shape and its image section gives the object’s depth along the axis (with the object distances of its near and far ends), the traced image depth, their ratio, \\(m_1m_2\\) from the lens equation at the two ends, and the traced height ratio of the tallest corner. A shape reading records these; a series of shape readings goes to the Depth table. Simulated data.",
"Measure where thin lenses form images, then use one lens as a magnifier and two lenses as a microscope and a telescope, and measure how much each magnifies.",
"A thin lens of focal length \\(f\\) (\\(f > 0\\) converging, \\(f < 0\\) diverging) forms the image of an object a distance \\(d_o\\) away at \\(d_i\\), with",
"\\[\\frac{1}{d_o} + \\frac{1}{d_i} = \\frac{1}{f}, \\qquad m = -\\frac{d_i}{d_o}.\\]",
"\\(d_i > 0\\) is a real image on the far side of the lens; \\(d_i < 0\\) is a virtual image on the object’s side. A camera is a converging lens that makes a real image on a sensor; to focus on a nearer object, the lens moves farther from the sensor.",
"<strong>Magnifier.</strong> The eye sees an object best at its near point, taken as {#} cm, where it fills an angle \\(\\theta_o = h/(25\\ \\mathrm{cm})\\). A converging lens held at the eye, with the object inside its focal length, makes an upright virtual image. That image fills the angle \\(\\theta_i = h_i/\\lvert d_i\\rvert = h/d_o\\), so the angular magnification is",
"\\[M = \\frac{\\theta_i}{\\theta_o} = \\frac{25\\ \\mathrm{cm}}{d_o}.\\]",
"With the image at the near point (\\(d_i = -25\\) cm) this is \\(M = 1 + (25\\ \\mathrm{cm})/f\\); with the image at infinity (object at \\(f\\), eye relaxed) it is \\(M = (25\\ \\mathrm{cm})/f\\).",
"<strong>Depth of an image.</strong> A lens makes images of points at different distances at different places, so an object with depth along the axis has an image with depth too. For two points of the object at \\(d_{o1}\\) and \\(d_{o2}\\), with magnifications \\(m_1\\) and \\(m_2\\), the thin lens equation gives exactly",
"\\[\\frac{\\lvert d_{i2}-d_{i1}\\rvert}{\\lvert d_{o2}-d_{o1}\\rvert} = m_1m_2,\\]",
"close to \\(m^2\\) for a thin object. So an image is stretched or squeezed along the axis by about the square of its height magnification.",
"<strong>Microscope.</strong> The objective makes a real, enlarged image with \\(m_{\\text{obj}} = -d_i/d_o\\), and the eyepiece is a magnifier for that image, \\(M_{\\text{eye}} = (25\\ \\mathrm{cm})/f_{\\text{eye}}\\) with the eye relaxed. The net magnification is \\(M_{\\text{net}} = m_{\\text{obj}} M_{\\text{eye}}\\).",
"<strong>Telescope.</strong> For a distant object, the objective’s image forms at its focal point, which is also the eyepiece’s focal point, so the lenses are \\(f_{\\text{obj}} + f_{\\text{eye}}\\) apart. The angular magnification is",
"\\[M = -\\frac{f_{\\text{obj}}}{f_{\\text{eye}}}.\\]",
"A converging lens has \\(f = 10\\) cm. Find the image distance and magnification for an object {#} cm from it, and for one {#} cm from it. Describe each image.",
"A diverging lens has \\(f = -10\\) cm. Find the image distance and magnification for an object {#} cm from it.",
"A magnifier has \\(f = 12\\) cm. Find \\(M\\) with the image at the near point and with the image at infinity. Where must the object be for the image to be at the near point?",
"A microscope has \\(f_{\\text{obj}} = 2.0\\) cm and \\(f_{\\text{eye}} = 5.0\\) cm, with the object {#} cm from the objective. Find \\(d_i\\), \\(m_{\\text{obj}}\\) and \\(M_{\\text{net}}\\). A telescope has \\(f_{\\text{obj}} = 16\\) cm and \\(f_{\\text{eye}} = 5.0\\) cm. Find \\(M\\) and the lens separation.",
"There are no hazards in the simulation. Never look at the Sun through a lens, telescope or camera viewfinder.",
"<strong>Part {#}: converging lens.</strong> In the Converging lens setup (\\(f = 10\\) cm), record with the object about {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} cm from the lens.",
"<strong>Part {#}: focusing a camera.</strong> With the object about {#} cm from the lens, move the screen until the picture on it is sharpest, and write the distance from the lens to the screen in the Notes for Part {#}. Repeat with the object about {#} cm away. (Nothing new is recorded.)",
"<strong>Part {#}: diverging lens.</strong> Make the lens diverging with \\(f = -10\\) cm. Record with the object about {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} cm from it.",
"<strong>Part {#}: magnifier.</strong> In the Magnifier setup (\\(f = 12\\) cm), record with the object about {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} cm from the lens. Then find the object distance that puts the image {#} cm from the lens (within {#} cm) and record.",
"<strong>Part {#}: microscope.</strong> Record the Microscope setup as it opens (\\(f_{\\text{obj}} = 2.0\\) cm, \\(f_{\\text{eye}} = 5.0\\) cm). Then change the eyepiece to \\(f_{\\text{eye}} = 2.5\\) cm, move it until the final image is at infinity, and record. Then put the eyepiece back to {#} cm, move the object to {#} cm from the objective, move the eyepiece until the final image is at infinity again, and record.",
"<strong>Part {#}: telescope.</strong> Record the Telescope setup as it opens (\\(f_{\\text{obj}} = 16\\) cm, \\(f_{\\text{eye}} = 5.0\\) cm). Then record with eyepieces of {#} and {#} cm, and with a {#} cm objective and the {#} cm eyepiece, each time with the lenses \\(f_{\\text{obj}} + f_{\\text{eye}}\\) apart.",
"<strong>Part {#}: depth of an image.</strong> In the A shape, point by point setup (\\(f = 8\\) cm), record with the house’s middle about {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} cm from the lens. Then record the A shape in a magnifier setup as it opens. Send this series; it goes to the Depth table.",
"<strong>Using the virtual apparatus.</strong> A microscope or telescope reading needs the rays leaving the eyepiece to be parallel; the section’s last line says when they are. Changing a focal length does not move the lens, so move it afterward. Send each part to the worksheet before clearing, so each part gets its own copy of the Data table. The last two columns are for you to fill in.",
